MAPDRV                                                         [3.0]
------

Format:      MAPDRV [/L] d: partition|d|u|s [device]
                    [slot[-subslot][:segment]]
             MAPDRV [/L] d: partition|d|u|s [device] [0]
             MAPDRV d: [/]filename [/RO]

Purpose:     Maps a drive to a partition of a device of a Nextor
        driver, or mounts a disk image file on a drive.

Use:         The first form maps the drive to the given partition  of
        the given device of the driver in the given slot  (which must
        be a Nextor driver).

             The partition can be any number starting at 1,  or 0  to
        map the drive directly to  the  device  (needed  for  devices
        without partitions,  such  as floppy disks).  Partition  num-
        bers 2 to 4 refer to the extended partitions 2-1 to 2-3  when
        partition 2 of the device is extended,  otherwise  they refer
        to primary partitions.

             Instead of a partition,  'd' reverts the  drive  to  its
        mapping at boot time and 'u' leaves the  drive  unmapped (any
        further arguments are ignored in both cases),  and    's'
        attaches the drive to the device without assigning  a  parti-
        tion:  a suitable one is searched on each access to the drive
        (useful for removable devices that are currently offline).

             The device must be a number from 1 to 255.

             The slot and subslot must be numbers from  0  to   3.
        A plain 0 with no subslot selects the slot  of  the   primary
        Nextor controller,  wherever it is; an explicit 0-0 means the
        literal slot 0-0.  The segment,  after a colon,  is only mea-
        ningful for drivers loaded in RAM.

             When device information is given but the slot  is  omit-
        ted, the drive is mapped to the given partition of the  given
        device of the driver already controlling  the  drive.    When
        both are omitted,  the drive is mapped to the given partition
        of the device it is already mapped to.  Both  cases   require
        the drive to be currently mapped to a Nextor driver.

             /L locks the drive (see HELP LOCK)  immediately  after a
        successful mapping.

             The second form mounts the given file on the  drive.   A
        file cannot be mounted on its own drive,  or on a drive  that
        contains a mounted file.  Prefix the file name with '/'  when
        it starts with a digit,  so that it is not taken for a parti-
        tion number; /RO mounts the file in read only mode.

             The same function is provided by the MAPDRV.COM external
        tool, which also works on older Nextor versions.

Examples:

        MAPDRV A: 2

             Drive A: is mapped to partition 2 of the device  it  is
        currently mapped to.

        MAPDRV /L E: 1 2 3-2

             Drive E: is mapped to partition 1 of device 2 of the
        Nextor driver in slot 3-2, and locked.

        MAPDRV B: 0 1 1:4

             Drive B: is mapped directly to device 1 of  the  Nextor
        driver loaded in RAM segment 4 whose master slot is 1.

        MAPDRV C: 3 1 0

             Drive C: is mapped to partition 3 of device  1  of  the
        primary Nextor controller.

        MAPDRV D: DISKS\GAMES.DSK /RO

             The disk image file GAMES.DSK is mounted read  only  on
        drive D:.

        MAPDRV G: u

             Drive G: is unmapped.
